No parallel governance system shall be created.

Existing effective authority shall be reused wherever sufficient.

This decision establishes Commander approval for a general delegated task-admission rule, subject to competent integration into the governing authority contract and valid prospective effectivity.

It also establishes the intended general integration operating model, without independently creating or expanding Grant C.

⸻

5. GENERAL DELEGATED TASK ADMISSION

Competent and authorized campaign functions shall have standing delegated authority to admit ordinary subordinate tasks within effectively authorized campaigns once this delegation has become operationally effective.

A task is eligible when:

1. It falls substantively within the campaign’s approved mission.
2. It remains within the approved scope and permitted effects.
3. The responsible function possesses the required authority.
4. Applicable technical and professional requirements are satisfied.
5. Required Build Hold coverage exists before restricted implementation.
6. No expressly reserved decision is required.
7. The campaign remains valid, effective and unrevoked.

Task admission shall be recorded through the ordinary campaign workflow with an identifiable connection to the controlling authority.

No separate Commander decision is required for each eligible routine task.

This provision is intended to establish actual delegated authority following valid effectivity, rather than merely express an organizational preference.

⸻

6. CAMPAIGN-SPECIFIC LIMITATIONS PREVAIL

General delegation shall never expand an individual campaign’s approved mandate.

The following take precedence over the general task-admission presumption:

* Express campaign-specific restrictions.
* Reserved decisions.
* Approved scope and effect boundaries.
* Expiry and revocation conditions.
* Applicable professional and technical authority limitations.
* Stricter effective governing requirements.

The absence of an explicit prohibition does not itself establish authority.

A task must be affirmatively covered by the effective campaign mandate and applicable delegation.

However, already established authority shall not require repeated substantive confirmation for every ordinary task.

⸻

7. DELEGATED PROFESSIONAL EXECUTION

Once a task has been validly admitted, competent authorized functions may execute the work within their effective grants.

Ordinary execution may include:

* Development and documentation.
* Technical problem-solving.
* Testing and validation.
* Bounded repairs.
* Pull-request preparation and updates.
* Review coordination.
* Resolution of qualified review findings.
* Preparation for authorized integration.

Existing effective AI professional execution simplifications shall apply.

A change of AI conversation, model or execution instance alone shall not trigger additional identity or authorization requirements where the effective governing contract already permits continuation under the same valid task, technical account and authority binding.

This provision does not create professional authority for an otherwise unauthorized actor.

⸻

8. GENERAL DELEGATED INTEGRATION MODEL

AUTHORIZED MERGE TO MAIN

Competent authorized integrators shall be able to merge qualified campaign changes into main without renewed substantive Commander approval when the exact integration action is covered by effective authority.

Every protected integration must satisfy:

1. Effective campaign authority.
2. Valid task admission.
3. Approved change scope.
4. Applicable Build Hold coverage.
5. Required tests and CI.
6. Mandatory independent review.
7. Exact PR HEAD and target-base verification.
8. Applicable currentness requirements.
9. Effective Grant C or other competent integration authorization.
10. Applicable per-PR disposition requirements.
11. Exact mutation authorization where required.
12. Branch protection and other mandatory controls.
13. Independent postmerge verification.

INTEGRATION AUTHORITY IS ACTION-SPECIFIC.

This order does not itself grant unrestricted merge authority.

It does not create, activate or expand Grant C.

Where an effective integration contract requires PR-specific authorization, that requirement remains binding.

Technical readiness, CI success and independent review do not independently establish merge authority.

Where effective delegated integration authority already covers the exact action, no additional Commander approval shall be demanded merely because integration is occurring.

Future competent amendments may establish broader campaign-level integration delegations without requiring a new organizational operating model.

⸻

9. INDEPENDENT CONTROLS

Mandatory controls remain in force.

No actor may self-approve work where independent review is required.

No actor may create its own missing authority through interpretation of this order.

Existing qualified evidence shall be reused when its object, scope, assumptions and currentness remain valid.

Reviews shall be proportionate to actual requirements and changes.

No unnecessary repeat review, new approval mechanism or additional administrative gate shall be introduced without a concrete governing requirement.

⸻

10. EXCEPTION-BASED COMMANDER ESCALATION

Human Commander shall be involved when a matter genuinely exceeds effective delegated authority or requires a reserved decision.

Examples include:

* Establishing a new campaign.
* Materially expanding an existing campaign.
* Creating or expanding professional or technical authority.
* Granting new Build Hold exceptions.
* Amending reserved governance requirements.
* Authorizing an integration not covered by effective delegated authority.
* Accepting risks reserved to Commander.
* Resolving authority conflicts that cannot be addressed by competent delegated functions.

Routine work already within effective authority shall proceed without escalation.

A NEW TASK IS NOT AUTOMATICALLY A NEW COMMANDER DECISION.

Escalation shall identify the exact missing authority or reserved decision.

⸻

11. STANDARD OPERATING SEQUENCE

All Rallyproof campaign functions shall apply the following sequence:

CAMPAIGN → SCOPE → AUTHORITY → ADMIT → EXECUTE → REVIEW → INTEGRATE → VERIFY

CAMPAIGN

Verify that the campaign is approved and effective.

SCOPE

Verify that the proposed task falls within the campaign mandate.

AUTHORITY

Verify competent delegation and applicable restrictions.

ADMIT

Admit the task and record its connection to effective authority.

EXECUTE

Complete authorized work.

REVIEW

Complete mandatory testing, qualification and independent verification.

INTEGRATE

Perform protected integration only when the exact merge action is authorized.

VERIFY

Independently verify and document the result.

OPERATING RULE:

When all applicable conditions are satisfied:

PROCEED.

When a concrete mandatory condition is missing:

STOP THE AFFECTED ACTION AND ESCALATE THE EXACT BLOCKER.

Do not restart already completed approval processes without relevant contrary evidence.

⸻

12. EXISTING AND FUTURE CAMPAIGNS

Following valid operational effectivity, this general delegation shall apply prospectively to existing and future effectively authorized campaigns, within their respective mandates.

Existing campaigns shall not require renewed substantive approval solely to adopt this general operating model.

Future campaigns shall be covered when their own authority becomes effective, without a separate adoption decision for each campaign.

Campaign-specific restrictions always prevail.

This order does not:

* Activate an ineffective campaign.
* Extend an expired campaign.
* Revive revoked authority.
* Expand an approved mandate.
* Grant new Build Hold exceptions.
* Authorize historical actions retroactively.
* Remove existing integration controls.

